 Description : Consolidation Short - S_Spring (PRODUCTION = Variant B: Path A on, Path B off)
 Version     : v14.6 + SetStopContract + SL_Pct (PRODUCTION; v14.5 fully removed after A/B failure)
               v14.3/v14.3.1 RangeForceExit was rolled back 2026-06-17;
               see docs/range_force_exit_rollback_20260617.md for rationale.
 Symbol      : TXF1 (Taiwan Stock Index Futures Front Month)
 Platform    : MultiCharts 9.0 PowerLanguage x64
 Timeframe   : Data1 = 15M / Data2 = 60M / Data3 = Daily

 CHANGELOG:
   v18.0-DIAG (2026-08-24) WHY DID CS_ReEntry FIRE ZERO TIMES.

          v18 with ReEntry_On = 1 produced 79 trades, all CS_Entry, byte
          identical to the anchor. The mechanism never fired once. This
          build finds out where it dies, by removing one condition per
          level and sweeping.

            ReEntry_Diag = 0   production (must reproduce 79 / 877,200)
                         = 1   DISARM disabled
                         = 2   1 + FIRE drops the structure gates
                               (v_is_in_consolidation, box valid,
                                Close < v_Trigger_Price, v_Risk_OK)
                         = 3   2 + FIRE drops the macro gates
                               (v_Trend_Dir, v_Macro_Block)
                         = 4   3 + ARM drops v_Trail_Active = false
                         = 5   4 + ARM drops ExitFired = 0

          The Rule #11 gates (Holiday / Night / Settlement) are NEVER
          removed at any level. Diagnosis does not get to break the
          calendar rules.

          READ IT AS A BISECTION. The first level whose trade count rises
          above 79 is the condition that was blocking. If even level 5
          stays at 79 then ARM itself never runs and the fault is in
          v_Prev_MP, the placement, or the state capture.

          LEADING HYPOTHESIS. The frozen stop sits at box_top + 2 x ATR,
          so being stopped out always means price traded ABOVE the box
          top -- and DISARM fires on exactly that. If the Data2 close
          also lands above the box top, DISARM clears the flag on the
          very bar ARM sets it. That is the same class of error caught in
          L1 design: the disarm condition being implied by the stop-out
          itself. I checked the TRIGGER against the stop and called the
          geometry clean; I did not check the DISARM against the stop.

          DIAGNOSTIC ONLY. Do not promote. Do not run with
          ReEntry_Diag > 0 as anything but a measurement.
   v18.0  (2026-08-24) RE-ENTRY. A second, parallel entry path.

          WHY NOW. v17's four risk-form switches are settled and all four
          ship at their v14.6 form: Run A (Stop_Form=2) lost in 29 of 29
          cells, Run D (Max_Entry_Risk_Pct) lost in 23 of 24, and both
          measurements pointed at the same structural fact -- this
          strategy's profit lives in the trades that carry the most risk
          at entry. So v18 changes NOTHING about risk. It adds re-entry
          and only re-entry.

          WHAT IT IS. Not folded into CS_Entry. A separate block with its
          own label CS_ReEntry, so the anchor can prove it inert and so
          its own win rate is measurable afterwards.

            ARM     the position went flat on a STOP (ExitFired = 0) while
                    the trail was NOT active. A trail exit is profit
                    taking, not an interrupted trade, so it does not arm.
            DISARM  Data2 close leaves the ORIGINAL box on either side.
                    Fail-safe: disarm is tested BEFORE fire.
            FIRE    Close <= the original entry price, with the macro and
                    Rule #11 gates re-checked.
            RESET   a CS_Entry fill starts a new cycle and clears the chain.

          CANDIDATE SET, MEASURED ON THE v17 ANCHOR: 11 trades out of 79,
          -518,000, which is 28.9 pct of gross loss. The 24 CS_SL winners
          are trail exits and are excluded by design; the 30 CS_BreakExit
          trades have a dead box thesis and must never arm. Eleven events
          over six and a half years will not settle anything statistically
          -- this is framework completion, not an edge claim.

          GEOMETRY. For a short the stop sits ABOVE entry and the re-entry
          trigger sits AT entry, separated by the stop distance. They can
          never coincide. L1 needed its arm anchored on the moving average
          for exactly this reason; L4 does not.

          STOP INHERITANCE. Going flat wipes v_SL_Locked and v_Frozen_ATR,
          so without this the re-entry would re-freeze on fresh ATR and
          could take MORE risk than the trade it resumes. MinList against
          the original keeps the tighter of the two (lower = tighter for
          a short). Mirror of L1 v32, which uses MaxList.

          TIME STOP. BarsSinceEntry restarts on the re-entry. User ruling
          2026-08-24: inheritance is about RISK parameters; the time stop
          asks whether this position is working, and a fresh position
          deserves a fresh clock.

          COOLDOWN. ReEntry_Cooldown_On = 0 by default, so Cooldown_Bars
          does not block the re-entry. Cooldown exists to stop the strategy
          re-arming into a failed setup; re-entry already carries a
          stricter gate of its own. Set to 1 to test the other way.

          NOT changed: the four v17 form switches, the CS_Entry conditions,
          the Priority 0-3 exit chain, ATR_Length, the box logic.

          Anchor: ReEntry_On = 0 must reproduce v17 trade for trade
          (79 / 877,200 / PF 1.4902 / MDD -789,200).
   v17.0  (2026-08-22) RISK FORM -- percentage alternatives behind switches.
          Ships with every switch on its CURRENT form, so the degeneracy
          anchor must reproduce live v14.6 trade for trade.

          WHY. The 2026-08-22 measurement found the loss size is already
          bounded -- median stop distance 0.41 to 0.53 pct of entry, single
          widest 1.50 pct landing exactly on the SL_Pct ceiling. What is NOT
          bounded is the money: 2 fixed lots against an index that went
          13,000 to 47,000 turns the same percentage into 3.6x the loss.
          User ruling: express the risk legs as percentages so the number
          that matters is fixed at entry, and gate the entry on it.

          Four switchable forms, all defaulting to the current behaviour:
            Buffer_Form  1 = Box_Top - ATR x mult      2 = Box_Top x (1-pct)
            Stop_Form    1 = LockedTop + ATR x mult    2 = Entry x (1+pct)
            Trail_Form   1 = Low + ATR x mult          2 = Low x (1+pct)
            Max_Entry_Risk_Pct = 0 disables the entry risk gate

          The entry risk gate is the piece that actually delivers "know the
          loss at entry": it computes the planned stop distance BEFORE the
          order and refuses the trade when it exceeds the cap. Failure
          direction is no-entry, which is fail-safe.

          NOT changed: ATR_Length, the box logic, CS_BreakExit, BE/SP (both
          ship at 0 and the file says do not enable without redesign).

          Measurement record: docs/research/L4_baseline_measurement_20260822.md
   v14.0  Initial S_Spring (False Breakout) live release with CS_Labels.

   v14.1  (2026-06-13) Holiday Flat Rule v3 + Frozen Initial SL.
          Net +12,000 / PF 1.602 -> 1.622 / MDD improved 6,600.
          CS_BreakExit 28/0pct/-545,200 left UNTOUCHED, flagged for v14.2.

   v14.2B (2026-06-13) PRODUCTION: Variant B sealed (Path A on, Path B off).
          A/B trial complete (variants A-G all backtested in MC9).
          See docs/L4_v142_variant_results.md for full data.

          WINNER: Variant B (Night_Block_On=true, BE=0, SP=0)
            Net  : 581,200 -> 687,200  (+106,000, +18.2 pct)
            PF   : 1.62 -> 1.83
            MDD  : -328,000 -> -239,800  (improved 88,200, -27 pct)
            Top-10 winner retention: 100 pct (no winners killed)
            Path A removes 9 night entries (net -120,800 in A)
            with ZERO BR cases; one bar leaks through at 02:00
            (signal fires at 01:45 = Time 145, outside 200-500
            block window) - net positive trade, acceptable.

          REJECTED variants:
            C (BE +60/+5): net -345K. CS_BE 25 trades 0 pct WR.
              Killed Top-10 winners: 4 of 10 became CS_BE.
              Worst kills: 2025-04-08 (+164K -> -1K),
              2020-03-13 (+140K -> -1K), 2022-07-01 (+68K -> -1K),
              2022-04-26 (+59K -> -1K). Total -435,800 stolen
              from top winners alone. L3 variant D pattern
              repeated: BE catches first retrace from MFE peak,
              missing multi-stage continuation.
            D (SP +80/50pct): net -212K. CS_SP 22 trades 100 pct
              WR (+235,200) - looked perfect in isolation. But
              CS_SL dropped from 45/+1,019,200 to 36/+618,600
              (-401K). SP truncates the multi-stage winners that
              eventually reach trail exit. Net winner.
            E (A+C), F (A+D), G (A+SP 100/50pct): A's gain
              cancelled by C/D/G's loss. All net negative vs A.

          KEY LESSON - L3 trap reborn in different clothing:
            L3 v13.2D failed because BE +50pts was below the
            normal range oscillation envelope.
            L4 v14.2 C/D fail because BE/SP at ANY threshold
            cuts the first retrace, and L4 big winners have
            multi-stage profit patterns (e.g. +60 -> +30 -> +200
            -> trail catches at +180). Both BE and SP catch the
            first retracement, losing the eventual big win.
            SP looks good because it has 100 pct WR on its own
            fires, but the systemic cost is killing CS_SL big
            winners that would have run further.

          PATH B CODE PRESERVED but inputs default to 0 (off).
          Future research could try SP with much higher trigger
          (+200pt) or post-trail-activation only - but for now
          BE/SP DISABLED is the empirical winner.

   v14.2  (2026-06-13) PATH A + PATH B A/B ENGINE (input-switched):

          THE PROBLEM ADDRESSED:
            v14.1 baseline has CS_BreakExit = 28 trades, 0 pct WR,
            -545,200 = ~94 pct of total net profit. The "false
            breakout" thesis failed in 28 cases; in 10 of those
            (avg MFE +97.5 pts, final -106.5 pts) the trade was
            briefly profitable before crashing - representing
            408,000 of recoverable wasted MFE.

          PATH A - Entry Quality Filter (diagnostic verified):
            Diagnostic finding: 02:00-04:59 entries = 9 trades,
            NET -120,800 (3 wins, 6 losses, ZERO CS_BreakExit).
            These are low-liquidity dead-of-night entries that
            simply lose money - none of them are BR cases.
            Filter cost: zero winner sacrificed.
            Filter benefit: +120,800 net (+20.8 pct vs v14.1).

          PATH B - Holding Period Protection (A/B trial):
            Diagnostic finding: of trades reaching MFE >= +60 pts,
            CS_SL avg final +198 pts (real winners, lots of room),
            CS_TimeExit avg +59 pts (modest winners),
            CS_BreakExit avg -108 pts (the failures).
            Only 8/50 (16 pct) of MFE>=60 trades end as BR.
            So a BE / SP floor at +60 pts threshold has a low
            risk of amputating real winners (their final is
            way above the floor).

          PATH B - Why this is NOT L3 variant D:
            L3 variant D failed at BE +50 pts because boxes
            oscillate +50 pts as normal noise. 100 CL_BE trades
            ended at 0 pct WR (96 amputated target hits).
            L4 v14.2 is structurally different:
              (1) +60 pts on TXF1 = ~3x ATR, well above noise.
              (2) MFE >= 60 trades resolve up (CS_SL +198)
                  or down (CS_BreakExit -108), not back to
                  range middle.
              (3) Reversal strategy has no "in-box target"
                  to amputate - the only outcomes are trend
                  continuation or break failure.

          INPUT SWITCHES (all default OFF = v14.1 behavior):
            Night_Block_On(false): blocks 02:00-04:59 entries
            BE_Trigger_Pts(0):    0 = off. Try 60/80/100.
            BE_Offset_Pts(5):     BE floor offset from entry
            SP_Trigger_Pts(0):    0 = off. Try 60/80/100.
            SP_Retain_Pct(50):    SP retention pct (0-100)

          NAMED VARIANTS FOR MC9 A/B TESTING:
            A baseline   : all off (= v14.1, regression check)
            B PathA only : Night=true, BE=0, SP=0
            C PathB BE   : Night=false, BE=60, SP=0
            D PathB SP   : Night=false, BE=0, SP=80/50
            E A+C        : Night=true, BE=60, SP=0
            F A+D        : Night=true, BE=0, SP=80/50  (expected best)
            G A+SP-conv  : Night=true, BE=0, SP=100/50 (safest)

          NEW EXIT LABELS:
            CS_BE  - stop hit at BE floor (Entry + Offset profit lock)
            CS_SP  - stop hit at SP floor (Entry + Peak*(1-Retain))
            CS_SL  - stop hit at original v_Stop_Level (when no
                     BE/SP floor was tighter)
            Floors apply by replacing the labeled Stop order, NOT
            by adding parallel orders. One Stop per bar, mutually
            exclusive priority: SP > BE > SL (tightest first).

          ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA (per variant):
            PASS: net+100K AND MDD <=v14.1 AND CS_BE/SP WR >=50 pct
                  AND Top-10 winner retention >=80 pct
            FAIL: CS_BE or CS_SP WR <30 pct (L3 trap), OR Top-10
                  retention <70 pct, OR MDD worse by >10 pct

          REFERENCES:
            docs/L4_v142_pathA_entry_diagnostic.md - 28 BR analysis
            docs/L4_v142_pathB_variant_matrix.md   - variant design

          DEPLOY ONLY WHEN FLAT (changes historical stop fills).
          Production defaults = all off = v14.1 (regression-safe).

 PERFORMANCE (v14.6 = PRODUCTION, sealed 2026-07-26):
   Net Profit    : 894,400 TWD
   Profit Factor : 1.54
